 ("p", "Harsh revenue targets and two crop failures caused the **Bengal famine of 1770-72**, killing about one-third of the people (about 10 million). W.W. Hunter described the horror. In the **Great Famine of 1876-78**, up to 8 million died; about one million tonnes of rice a year were exported, 'free market' policy stopped price control (Lord Lytton), and relief was inadequate while a grand durbar was held in Delhi. Total famine deaths under British rule are estimated at 50 to 100 million. Earlier famines occurred but never on such a scale."),
